Packages that necorouter needs on the target system.
The installer (install.sh) attempts to install the listed packages (best effort).
It does not perform a full operating-system upgrade, though the package manager may update packages needed by the router.
Package names were chosen for typical distributions and have not been verified on real systems:
check them against your repository.

These are runtime dependencies, not the installer's download tools. For GitHub releases, install.sh uses an
available curl, wget, BusyBox wget, or Python 3. If none is available and dependency installation is enabled,
it tries to install curl through the system package manager. The necorouter binary is checked against SHA256SUMS
from the same release; this detects transfer damage, not a compromised release.

Dependency package signatures are checked by the distribution's package manager according to its repository
configuration. APT authenticates signed repository metadata by default, DNF follows each repository's gpgcheck
setting, and Alpine APK uses trusted signing keys. The installer does not maintain a separate hash list for packages.

Purpose                            Debian/Ubuntu               RHEL/RedOS/Fedora        Alpine
---------------------------------  --------------------------  -----------------------  ---------------------
sshd (login, banner)               openssh-server              openssh-server           openssh
nftables (NAT, filter-map, ...)    nftables                    nftables                 nftables
DHCP server, DNS                   dnsmasq-base                dnsmasq                  dnsmasq
OSPF                               frr                         frr (EPEL/repository     frr
                                                               FRR)
NTP client                         chrony                      chrony                   chrony
Time zones                         tzdata                      tzdata                   tzdata
ping, traceroute                   iputils-ping traceroute     iputils traceroute       iputils traceroute
network / traffic shaping         iproute2 (ip, tc, IFB)     iproute (ip, tc, IFB)    iproute2 (ip, tc, IFB)
IPsec (IKEv1/IKEv2)          strongswan-swanctl          strongswan               strongswan
                                   strongswan-charon

Notes
- The router starts charon lazily: only when the configuration contains crypto-ipsec. IPsec is needed only for the crypto-ipsec / crypto-map commands. Without strongSwan these commands
  are accepted, but the service does not start: show services and the warning on apply
  will say "strongSwan is not installed".
- The router starts charon itself (like frr, dnsmasq, chrony); the system strongswan service
  does not need to be enabled, and it is best to disable it when installing the package: systemctl disable --now strongswan
  (or strongswan-starter / strongswan-swanctl, depending on the distribution).
- The charon and swanctl binaries are required. Search directories: PATH, /usr/lib/ipsec, /usr/libexec/ipsec,
  /usr/lib/strongswan, /usr/libexec/strongswan, /usr/sbin, /sbin, /usr/local/sbin.
- Network configuration uses `ip` from the listed iproute package for links, addresses, routes and IFB. Traffic
  policies use `tc` from the same package and the Linux CAKE qdisc (`sch_cake`); kernel support depends on the
  distribution and kernel build. Shaping is applied to a service-instance only when a service-policy with a
  bandwidth is attached in the `in` or `out` direction.
